Yes. What you’re describing is basically comparative morphology for institutions: stop comparing labels like “corporation,” “state,” or “church,” and instead compare the functions, organs, flows, pressures, and adaptations underneath them.

The key move is:

Different forms can be homologous if they solve the same functional problem.

A government ministry, a corporate department, and a religious hierarchy may look culturally unrelated while occupying almost the same structural niche.

I’d make this a Pantheon operator called Functional Morphology.

The conserved functions

Most durable human institutions eventually need some version of the same machinery:

Function	Business	Government	Religion
Acquire resources	Revenue, investment	Taxes, borrowing	Donations, tithes, land
Allocate resources	Budgets, capex	Appropriations	Ministries, charity, buildings
Establish legitimacy	Brand, results, ownership	Law, elections, tradition	Doctrine, revelation, lineage
Coordinate people	Management	Administration	Clergy / community leadership
Set rules	Policies, contracts	Laws, regulation	Doctrine, codes, customs
Enforce rules	HR, firing, litigation	Police, courts, sanctions	Discipline, exclusion, social pressure
Gather information	Analytics, sales data	Census, intelligence, bureaucracy	Confession, pastoral networks, community observation
Recruit	Hiring, marketing	Citizenship, immigration, political coalition	Conversion, birth, evangelism
Train	Onboarding	Schools, academies, civil service	Catechesis, seminaries, ritual
Maintain identity	Corporate culture	National identity	Shared belief and ritual
Reproduce leadership	Promotions, succession	Elections, appointments	Ordination, succession
Handle deviation	Performance management	Criminal/civil process	Heresy, reform, schism
Adapt	Product innovation	Policy reform	Interpretation, revival, doctrinal development

Already you can see the web.

The surface vocabulary changes while the underlying organs recur.

